On a transverse coordinate chart $(u,v)$ about a general point of $E$, with $E=(u=0)$, write a generator of the lifted Hodge line as $$\label{altord:h:nilpotent-expression}
 e^{zN}a(u,v),\qquad z=\frac{\log u}{2\pi i},$$ where $a$ is holomorphic across $u=0$ and $a(0,v)\ne0$. After shrinking the chart there is an integer $\ell\ge0$ such that $$\label{altord:h:primitive-line}
 N^{\ell+1}a(0,v)=0,\qquad N^\ell a(0,v)\ne0,$$ and the line belongs to weight $w+\ell$ in the limiting mixed Hodge structure. Moreover, $$\label{altord:h:boundary-rank}
 \nu(J|_E)\le
 \mathop{\mathrm{rk}}_{\mathrm{gen}}d\bigl(v\longmapsto[N^\ell a(0,v)]\bigr).$$ The map on the right is formed in the chosen flat coordinates. If $N=0$, take $\ell=0$; the variation then extends purely near the general point of $E$.

Let $g_0:X_0\to Y_0$ be an algebraic fibre space between smooth projective varieties, with geometric generic fibre of Kodaira dimension zero. After birational modifications of its source and base there is a commutative diagram $$\begin{array}{ccc}
 X'&\xrightarrow{\alpha}&X_0\\
 {\scriptstyle g}\downarrow&&\downarrow{\scriptstyle g_0}\\
 Y&\xrightarrow{\beta}&Y_0
\end{array}$$ with $X'$ and $Y$ smooth, and rational divisors $B,M,R$ such that $$\label{altord:cb:formula}
 K_{X'}\sim_{\mathbf Q}g^*(K_Y+B+M)+R.$$ Here $B\geq0$, its support has simple normal crossings, $(Y,B)$ is klt, and $M$ is nef. Moreover, $$\label{altord:cb:residual}
 g_*\mathcal O_{X'}(\lfloor kR^+\rfloor)=\mathcal O_Y
 \quad(k\in\mathbf Z_{>0}),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{codim}_Y g(\mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}R^-)\geq2,$$ and $R^-$ is $\alpha$-exceptional. The divisor $M$ is the trace of the moduli b-divisor, which can be assumed to descend to $Y$. On a sufficiently high generically finite base change admitting semistable reduction, its pullback is $\mathbf Q$-linearly equivalent to a positive rational multiple of the Schmid extension of the distinguished canonical Hodge eigensheaf of the canonical root cover.

The numerical dimension of the rational top Hodge line equals the generic rank of its line period map: $$\nu(L)=\mathop{\mathrm{rank}}(d\ell)_{\mathrm{gen}}.$$ In the unipotent case, if $d=\dim P$, $\omega$ is a smooth Kähler form, and $\alpha$ is the first Chern form of the Hodge metric on $L|_{P^\circ}$, then, for $0\le k\le d$, $$\label{p1logalt:chern}
 \int_{P^\circ}\alpha^k\wedge\omega^{d-k}
 =c_1(L)^k\cdot[\omega]^{d-k}.$$ The integrals are absolutely convergent.
